    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a section for my food science thesis regarding thermal transformations in proteinaceous foods. Can you explain the specific biochemical role of amino acids and reducing sugars in the Maillard reaction, and how modulating the pH of the environment (e.g., adding an alkaline agent) impacts the rate of these non-enzymatic browning reactions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Amino acids act as the nucleophilic donor of the amino group, while reducing sugars provide the carbonyl group, initiating the reaction to form Schiff bases and subsequent advanced glycation end products. Increasing the pH by adding an alkaline agent accelerates this process by deprotonating the amino group, creating a stronger nucleophile that reacts more readily with the sugar. Consequently, higher pH levels create optimal conditions for rapid non-enzymatic browning and color development in proteinaceous foods.",
      "source": "target",
